maps ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  mGender group:ngdraMen2lcdgt  mNationality/regional group:nnataNew Yorkers (New York State)2lcdgt  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  aMaps on endpapers.00tPrologue: The lessons of battle --tGeneral Jackson's Protégé --tGone to Texas --t"Come and Take It" --tConcepción --tA Slow Siege at the Alamo --tThe Defenders --tTwelve Days of Uncertainty --tThe Massacre --tBring Out the Dead --tHouston Hears the News --tFort Defiance --tThe Texian Exodus --tAn Army Assembles --tThe Battle at San Jacinto --t"Remember the Alamo!" --tOld Sam Jacinto --tPresident Sam Houston --tEpilogue: The Founding and the Founders of Texas.  aMarch 1836: The story of the Alamo is familiar to most: more than two hundred Texians trapped in an adobe mission, and massacred. Though the rallying cry of "Remember the Alamo" rang across the country, Houston knew it was poor strategy to aggressively retaliate immediately. One month after the massacre, he and his army of underdog Texians soundly defeated Santa Anna's troops in under eighteen minutes at the Battle of San Jacinto, and in doing so won the independence for which so many had died. Kilmeade brings one of the most pivotal moments in American history to life. -- adapted from jacket  aIngrambShelf ready books10aHouston, Sam,d1793-1863.10aSanta Anna, Antonio López de,d1794-1876. 0aTexasxHistoryyRevolution, 1835-1836. 0aSan Jacinto, Battle of, Tex., 1836. 0aAlamo (San Antonio, Tex.)xSiege, 1836. 0aGovernorszTexasvBiography. 0aLegislatorszUnited StatesvBiography. 0aTexasxHistoryyTo 1846. 1aTexas 1aTexaszSan Antonio. 1aTexasxHistory. 7aHISTORYzUnited Statesx19th Century. 7aUnited StatesxHistoryxState & LocalxSouthwest (AZ, NM, OK, TX) 7aLegislators. 7aAlamo (San Antonio, Tex.), Battle of the, 1836. 7aBattle of San Jacintoc(Texas :d1836)2fast0(OCoL